---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, ADS_ENABLED, ANALYTICS_CONFIGURED, NEWSLETTER_ENABLED, withBase } from '../consts';
const updated = '9 November 2026';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What AI Audio Report collects, which is close to nothing: no cookies by default, no analytics without consent, no accounts, no mailing list, no data sold."
>
  <h1 class="page-title">Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="meta">Last updated {updated}</p>

  <aside class="note">
    <strong>Short version</strong>
    <p>
      I set no cookies unless you say yes. There are no accounts and no
      trackers{ADS_ENABLED ? ', and advertising loads only after you consent' : ' or advertising'}.
      {NEWSLETTER_ENABLED
        ? ' There is a newsletter, and you are only on it if you asked to be.'
        : ' There is no mailing list.'}
      I sell nothing to anybody. My hosting provider sees your IP address, because that is
      how the web works.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    This site is run by an individual, Arnau Gran i Romero, who is the data controller for the
    purposes of the UK and EU GDPR. Contact:
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is collected, and when</h2>

  <h3>By default: nothing I can identify you from</h3>
  <p>
    AI Audio Report is a set of static files. Visiting it creates no account, sets no cookie of
    mine, and stores nothing about you on my side. There is no contact form, no comment
    system and no login. The newsletter sign-up is the only field on the site, and it stores
    nothing until you type an address into it.
  </p>

  <h3>By my host</h3>
  <p>
    The site is served by GitHub Pages (GitHub, Inc.). Like any web server, it processes your
    IP address and request metadata in order to deliver the page, and it may retain that in
    logs for security and abuse prevention. I do not have access to those logs and cannot
    query them. GitHub's handling of that data is governed by its own privacy statement.
  </p>

  <h3>Analytics, only with consent</h3>
  <p>
    I would like to know which pages are worth writing more of. If, and only if, you press
    Accept on the cookie banner, Google Analytics 4 is loaded and sets its own cookies. It is
    configured with IP anonymisation on, and with advertising storage, ad personalisation and
    ad user data all explicitly denied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Press Reject, or ignore the banner, and no analytics script is ever fetched. Nothing is
    loaded before you choose: this is a genuine prior-consent gate, not a banner that tracks
    you while it asks.
    {!ANALYTICS_CONFIGURED && ' Analytics is not configured at the moment, so even Accept currently loads nothing.'}
  </p>
  <p>
    The only thing stored in your browser by me is a single <code>localStorage</code> entry
    recording your answer, so I stop asking. It contains the word “granted” or “denied” and
    nothing else, it is not a cookie, and it is never transmitted anywhere. Details on the
    <a href={withBase('/cookies/')}>cookies page</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>If you email me</h3>
  <p>
    I receive your address and whatever you write, in order to reply. The legal basis is my
    legitimate interest in answering correspondence. It is not shared with anyone, and it is
    never added to the newsletter: that only happens if you subscribe yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>What I never do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sell, rent or trade personal data. There is no scenario in which this changes.</li>
    <li>
      {ADS_ENABLED
        ? 'Run retargeting pixels, fingerprinting or session recording. Advertising is served by Google AdSense and only after you consent.'
        : 'Run advertising cookies, retargeting pixels, fingerprinting or session recording.'}
    </li>
    <li>Load third-party fonts, embeds or widgets that would disclose your IP to someone else.</li>
    <li>Use dark patterns to extract consent. Reject is as easy to press as Accept.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The newsletter</h3>
  {NEWSLETTER_ENABLED ? (
    <>
      <p>
        If you subscribe, your email address is stored in order to send you the newsletter,
        and for nothing else. The legal basis is your consent, which you give by subscribing
        and can withdraw at any time using the unsubscribe link in every email. I do not
        sell, rent or share the list, and nobody who merely writes to me is ever added.
      </p>
      <p>
        The newsletter is operated for me by <strong>Buttondown, LLC</strong>, which acts as
        my processor and stores subscriber addresses in the United States. That transfer is
        covered by the Standard Contractual Clauses, incorporated into Buttondown's data
        processing agreement, which forms part of its service agreement. Buttondown is
        therefore a recipient of your address, and you can read how it handles data in its
        own privacy policy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subscribing uses confirmed opt-in: you receive an email asking you to confirm, and
        you are not added to the list until you do. If you never confirm, nothing further is
        sent to you.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <p>
      There is no newsletter and no mailing list. Nothing on this site collects an email
      address. If that changes, this policy is updated before the first address is collected,
      naming the provider that would process it.
    </p>
  )}

  <h3>Advertising</h3>
  {ADS_ENABLED ? (
    <p>
      This site carries advertising from Google AdSense. The ad script is requested only
      after you press Accept, and Google may then set its own cookies to serve and measure
      ads and to cap repetition. Google acts as an independent controller for that
      processing. Reject means the script is never fetched.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p>
      No advertising runs on this site today, and no ad script or advertising cookie is
      requested whichever button you press. An application to Google AdSense is under
      review. If advertising is ever switched on, this policy and the
      <a href={withBase('/cookies/')}> cookies page</a> are updated before the change, not
      after it.
    </p>
  )}
  <p>
    Separately, note that serving personalised advertising to visitors in the EEA and the UK
    requires a certified consent platform integrated with the IAB Transparency and Consent
    Framework. The banner used here is my own and is not certified, which limits what could
    be served even if advertising were enabled.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR you may request access to, correction of, or erasure of personal data I
    hold about you, object to processing, and request portability. Realistically, the only
    personal data I am likely to hold is an email you sent me, and you can have it deleted
    by asking. Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You also have
    the right to complain to your national data protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Transfers and retention</h2>
  <p>
    My host operates internationally, so serving a page may involve processing outside the
    EEA. Email correspondence is retained only as long as it is useful for the conversation it
    belongs to. Analytics data, once it exists, is retained for the shortest period Google
    Analytics allows me to set.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes materially, the date at the top changes and the substance of the
    change is described here rather than silently swapped in.
  </p>
  <p class="meta">
    9 November 2026: rewritten in the first person to match the rest of the site, and one line
    corrected that still said there was no newsletter sign-up. Nothing changed about what is
    collected, who processes it, or what you can ask me to delete.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
